  Data: {
  "authors": "Renjini Lalitha, Matthew Hicks, Mosarrat Qureshi, Kumar Kumaran",
  "date": "2024",
  "documentType": "retrospective case-control study",
  "summary": "This study investigates risk factors for Bell's Stage III necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC-III) in preterm neonates <33 weeks gestation, focusing on umbilical arterial catheter (UAC) duration. NEC-III occurred earlier (13.7 vs 23.9 days) and more severely than Stage II NEC, with UAC >5 days and prolonged rupture of membranes emerging as significant predictors.",
  "methods": "• Single-center retrospective case-control study (2015-2018) in a Canadian tertiary NICU\n• Cases: neonates with NEC-III; controls: NEC-II (1:1) and non-NEC (1:2-4) matched by GA ±1 week and birth date ±3 months\n• Data sources: neonatal database & chart review for maternal/neonatal demographics, perinatal course, feeding, respiratory support, sepsis, PDA, vasoactive agents, umbilical line presence/duration/malposition\n• Analytical methods: descriptive statistics, χ²/Fisher exact tests, one-way ANOVA/Student’s t tests; multivariate logistic regression (forward/backward stepwise) to identify risk factors for NEC-III vs NEC-II and non-NEC controls; significance p<0.05",
  "findings": "• Primary outcomes: UAC >5 days independently associated with NEC-III (AOR 3.8, 95% CI 1.05-13.66 vs non-NEC; AOR 5.57, 95% CI 1.65-18.73 vs NEC-II, p=0.006); ROM >1 week also significant (AOR 6.93 vs non-NEC; 11.74 vs NEC-II)\n• NEC-III neonates had lower GA (25.4 vs 27.3 weeks NEC-II, p=0.0003), earlier onset (13.7 vs 23.9 days, p=0.01), and higher SNAPPE-II illness severity (47.5 vs 28.4 NEC-II, p=0.003)\n• Conclusion: Prolonged UAC duration is a modifiable risk factor for NEC-III; limiting UAC placement may aid prevention, warranting prospective validation and policy review.",
  "markdown": "|\n\nDOI: 10.1002/jpr3.12081\n\n## ORIGINAL ARTICLE\n\nGastroenterology\n\n## Umbilical arterial catheter duration as risk factor for Bell's Stage III necrotizing enterocolitis in preterm neonates\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n## Renjini Lalitha\n\n## 1 | Matthew Hicks 2 | Mosarrat Qureshi 2 | Kumar Kumaran 2\n\n1 Department of Pediatrics, Division of Neonatal -Perinatal Medicine, University of Western Ontario, London, Ontario, Canada\n\n2 Department of Pediatrics, Division of Neonatal -Perinatal Medicine, Edmonton, Alberta, Canada\n\n## Correspondence\n\nRenjini Lalitha, The Division of Neonatal -Perinatal Medicine, London Health Sciences Centre, 800 Commissioners Rd. East, London, ON N6A 5W9, Canada. Email: Renjini.Lalitha@lhsc.on.ca\n\n## Funding information None\n\n## 1 | I NTRODUCTION\n\nNecrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) is a serious intestinal in /uniFB02 ammatory disease in neonates /uniFB01 rst described in 1965 by Mizrahi et al. 1 The disease is characterized by in /uniFB02 ammation and injury of the gut wall barrier that may\n\n## Abstract\n\nMethods: This was a retrospective case -control study of neonates born &lt;33 weeks gestational age (GA) who were admitted to a tertiary neonatal intensive care unit, between 2015 and 2018. NEC -III cases were compared with Stage -II NEC (NEC -II) and non -NEC controls. Two to four non -NEC controls were matched by GA ± 1 week and date of birth ± 3 months, to one NEC -III case. Univariate and multivariate analyses were used to examine risk factors for NEC -III.\n\nObjectives: To explore risk factors for Stage -III necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC -III) in preterm neonates.\n\nResults: Of 1360 neonates born &lt;33 weeks, 71 (5.2%) had NEC -II and above, with 46% being NEC -III. Mean age of onset of NEC -III was 13.7 days versus 23.9 days for NEC -II ( p = 0.01). Neonates with NEC -III were of lower GA (NEC -III 25.4 weeks, NEC -II 27.3 weeks, and non -NEC 26 weeks; p = 0.0008) and had higher Score for Neonatal Acute Physiology Perinatal Extension -II scores (NEC -III 47.5, NEC -II 28.4 and non -NEC 37, p = 0.003). Multivariate analysis showed duration of umbilical arterial catheter (UAC) &gt;5 days was signi /uniFB01 cantly associated with the development of NEC -III with adjusted odds ratio (AOR) 3.8; 95% con /uniFB01 dence interval (CI) (1.05 -13.66) for NEC -III versus non -NEC and AOR 5.57; 95% CI (1.65 -18.73), p = 0.006 for NEC -III versus NEC -II. Rupture of membranes (ROM) &gt;1 week was associated with NEC -III (AOR 6.93; 95% CI [1.56 -30.69] vs. non -NEC and AOR 11.74; 95% CI [1.14 -120.34] vs. NEC -II).\n\nConclusion: The increased association of NEC -III with duration of UAC and ROM could be further examined in prospective studies, and an upper limit for UAC duration could be considered in NEC prevention bundles.\n\n## KEYWORDS\n\nnecrotizing enterocolitis, neonates, preterm, risk factors, umbilical arterial catheter advance to necrosis and potentially, perforation of the gut. 2,3 NEC occurs in 5% -10% of very low birth weight neonates (VLBW). 4,5\n\nNEC is categorized into three stages based on modi /uniFB01 ed Bell's staging criteria, namely, Stage I -suspected NEC, Stage II proven NEC, and Stage III\n\nThis is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.\n\n© 2024 The Author(s). JPGN Reports published by Wiley Periodicals LLC on behalf of The European Society for Pediatric Gastroenterology, Hepatology, and Nutrition and the North American Society for Pediatric Gastroenterology, Hepatology, and Nutrition.\n\n|\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nAdvanced NEC based on severity of clinical signs and symptoms, radiological /uniFB01 ndings and laboratory abnormalities as described in Appendix 1. 6,7 The clinical presentation of NEC can be highly variable mimicking sepsis but the presence of pneumatosis intestinalis and/or portal venous gas on X -rays or necrotic bowel at the time of surgery helps to differentiate NEC stage II or above from sepsis. While stage I NEC includes patients with mildest symptoms, radiological signs of pneumatosis intestinalis con /uniFB01 rm Stage II NEC. Stage III NEC (NEC -III) is the severe form and reported to comprise 20% -40% of all cases. There is a high mortality rate with signi /uniFB01 cant morbidity in survivors. 8,9 NEC -III not only shows all the clinical and radiological signs of stage I and II NEC but also deterioration of vital signs, presence of shock, coagulopathies, bowel necrosis, and pneumoperitoneum. Several risk factors are associated with the development of NEC. 4,10 -12 The most consistent epidemiologic risk factors for NEC have been identi /uniFB01 ed as prematurity and enteral feeding practices. 13,14\n\nMore recently, Sulemanji and group, through their observational study found the presence of an umbilical venous catheter (UVC) into the portal vein or ductus venosus was associated with an increased incidence of NEC in VLBW neonates. 15 While there is some evidence showing impaired splanchnic circulation with umbilical artery catheterization (UAC), 16,17 there are con /uniFB02 icting results on its presence or tip position and associated development of NEC. 18,19 Although Thompson et al. looked at risk factors for NEC -totalis (de /uniFB01 ned as greater than 80% intestinal necrosis), 20 there have been no studies looking at risk factors for NEC -III in general.\n\n## 1.1 | Study rationale\n\nNEC -III has a high case fatality rate and is associated with prolonged hospital stay, total parenteral nutrition dependence, liver dysfunction, neurodevelopmental disabilities, growth failure, short bowel syndrome, intestinal scarring, and need for surgical intervention. 8,9 To date there have been no study comparisons assessing risk factors for the development of NEC -III, and speci /uniFB01 cally, its association with duration of umbilical line placement. The aim of this exploratory study is to develop a speci /uniFB01 c hypothesis for the prediction of NEC -III development in preterm neonates, which could be tested in future research.\n\n## 1.2 | Objectives\n\n1. To assess the risk factors associated with the development of NEC -III in preterm neonates born less than 33 weeks gestational age (GA).\n\n## What is Known\n\n- Stage -III necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC -III) has high mortality and morbidity among all types of NEC. Risk factors for the development of NEC in general have been documented in many studies but those for NEC -III in particular remain unexplored.\n\n## What is New\n\n- NEC -III occurs earlier compared to NEC -II suggesting a unique predisposing factor to its development.\n- Duration of umbilical artery catheterization (UAC) greater than 5 days was associated with the development of NEC -III.\n- An upper limit for the duration of UAC might be warranted for the prevention of NEC.\n2. To investigate the association of the presence and duration of umbilical catheterization with the development of NEC -III in preterm neonates born less than 33 weeks GA.\n\n## 1.3 | De /uniFB01 nitions\n\n1. NEC was scored according to the following criteria: (a) de /uniFB01 nite pneumatosis (air within the bowel wall) or portal/hepatic gas as diagnosed by x -ray or ultrasonography or (b) surgical or autopsy diagnosis of NEC. Diagnosis of ' suspected NEC ' or x -rays showing pneumoperitoneum without pneumatosis will not be classi /uniFB01 ed as NEC. NEC cases were staged as per Modi /uniFB01 ed Bell's staging criteria (Supporting Information S1: Appendix S1).\n2. Vasoactive agents are agents that increase myocardial contractility (inotropes) and/or increase vascular tone like vasopressors. The Vasoactive Inotrope score quanti /uniFB01 es the amount of cardiovascular therapy using a weighted sum of all the inotropic and vasopressor agents (Supporting Information S1: Appendix S2).\n\n## 2 | METHODS\n\nThis was a single -center retrospective case -control study conducted at a level three neonatal intensive care unit (NICU) in West Canada between January 2015 and December 2018 using a combination of neonatal database and chart review This center is one of the largest tertiary perinatal centers in Canada with a 69 bed NICU that receives approximately 1300\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n|\n\n|\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nadmissions annually. Neonates born less than 33 weeks who were managed as NEC -III during the study period formed the case group and those with NEC -II and without NEC formed the two control groups. The NEC -III cases were compared with NEC -II (1NEC -III: 1NEC -II) and with non -NEC controls (1 NEC -III case: 2 -4 non -NEC) that were matched for GA ± 1 week, and date of birth within 3 months. Neonates with lethal congenital malformations, chromosomal/genetic abnormalities, congenital heart defects (except for patent ductus arteriosus, ventricular septal defects, and atrial septal defects), and intestinal anomalies were excluded. All cases of NEC that were managed in the NICU were reviewed in detail by the local NEC working group committee consisting of neonatologists, neonatal fellows, radiologists, nurse practitioners, nurses, nutritionists, and clinical educators. The diagnosis of NEC and its stages were con /uniFB01 rmed by this committee, using Modi /uniFB01 ed Bell's staging criteria (Supporting Information S1: Appendix S1).\n\n## 2.1 | Data collection\n\nWe conducted extensive chart and database review for maternal and neonatal demographics, and perinatal data such as GA, birth weight, sex, antenatal steroid/ magnesium sulfate, pregnancy -related morbidities, mode of delivery, Apgar score, surfactant use, and cord gases. Data on feeding, vasoactive agents, respiratory support, sepsis, presence of patent ductus arteriosus (PDA), need for nonsteroidal anti -in /uniFB02 ammatory drugs (NSAIDS) for PDA treatment, presence and duration of umbilical lines, and presence of malpositioned umbilical lines before diagnosis of NEC in both NEC -III and NEC -II groups or within 60 days of life for non -NEC group were also collected.\n\n## 2.2 | Statistical analysis\n\nGiven the retrospective and exploratory nature of the study, an a priori sample size calculation was not performed and a sample of convenience was used. All statistical analyses were performed with Intercooled Stata Version 15.0 (StataCorp). All data were anonymized for analysis. Groups within the study were compared using descriptive statistical methods. Maternal and neonatal characteristics were compared between groups using Pearson's χ 2 and Fisher's exact tests for categorical variables and one -way analysis of variance (for comparing three groups -NEC -III, NEC -II, and non -NEC) or Student's t test for comparing continuous variables. Histograms and boxplots were used to assess underlying assumptions of normal distribution and normality of variances. Multivariate logistic regression models were built to identify risk factors associated with the development of NEC -III compared to NEC -II and controls using a forward and backward stepwise selection approach and variables were included if they had a p value less than 0.2 in bivariate analysis. Associations between variables and outcomes were expressed as adjusted odds ratios (AORs) and 95% con /uniFB01 dence intervals (CIs). A p value &lt; 0.05 was considered signi /uniFB01 cant. Adjustments for multiple comparisons were not made given the exploratory nature of this work. Unmatched analyses are presented. This study was approved by the Northern Alberta Clinical Trials and Research Center and the Human Research Ethics Of /uniFB01 ce at the University of Alberta.\n\n## 3 | RESULTS\n\nA total of 1360 preterm neonates born less than 33 weeks were admitted to our center during the 4 -year study period. The incidence of NEC -II and NEC -III was 5.2% in VLBW, with 46% of cases meeting the criteria for NEC -III (Figure 1). Neonates with NEC -III were of lower GA (25.4 ± 2.3 weeks) compared to NEC -II (27.3 ± 2.7 weeks) ( p = 0.0003, data not shown). Average age of onset of NEC -III was 13.7 (12.2 standard deviation [SD]) days versus 23.9 (18.8 SD) days for NEC -II ( p = 0.01).\n\nTable 1 compares the demographic, clinical, and biochemical characteristics of the NEC -III, NEC -II, and non -NEC groups. Severity of illness was de /uniFB01 ned using the Score for Neonatal Acute Physiology Perinatal Extension -II (SNAPPE -II score). Neonates with NEC -III had a higher severity of illness (SNAPPE -II 47.5; SD 25.1) than NEC -II infants (SNAPPE -II 28.4; SD 19.5) and non -NEC infants (SNAPPE -II 37; SD 22.1), ( p =0.003). Infants who developed NEC -III were on vasoactive agents longer (7.5 days vs. 3.2 days and 4.2 days for NEC -III, NEC -II, and non -NEC, respectively; p =0.09), although this was not statistically signi /uniFB01 cant. There were no signi /uniFB01 cant differences in the baseline lactate/maximum metabolic de /uniFB01 cits after birth, need for normal saline bolus, and peak vasoactive inotrope scores within the /uniFB01 rst 3 days of life between groups. Rates of antenatal steroids exposure and Apgar scores &lt;7 at 10 min were not statistically signi /uniFB01 cant among study groups.\n\nTable 1 also compares feeding characteristics between the groups. The timing of /uniFB01 rst enteral feeds was not statistically different between the groups. The majority of neonates with NEC -III never achieved full enteral feeds before the development of NEC (60.6% in NEC -III vs. 36.3% in NEC -II, p ≤ 0.0001). Among those who achieved full enteral feeds, the mean days to full feeds did not differ between the groups.\n\nThe presence, position, and duration of both umbilical lines were analyzed between NEC -III, NEC -II, and non -NEC groups as shown in Table 2. The rate\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n|\n\nFIGURE 1 Study /uniFB02 ow diagram. GA, gestational age; NEC -I, stage -I necrotizing enterocolitis; NEC -II, stage -II necrotizing enterocolitis; NEC -III, stage -III necrotizing enterocolitis; NICU, neonatal intensive care unit; SCH, Stollery Philip C. Etches Hospital.\n\nThe image presents a flowchart illustrating the process of patient selection for a study on Necrotizing Enterocolitis (NEC) in neonates. The chart outlines the steps taken to identify and categorize patients based on their condition.\n\n* **Initial Patient Pool**\n\t+ 1360 neonates under 33 weeks admitted to SCH-NICU from January 2015 onwards\n* **Patient Chart Request**\n\t+ 182 patient charts requested\n\t+ 15 charts excluded due to:\n\t\t- 3 transferred with established NEC-II\n\t\t- 4 significant missing information for NEC-II\n\t\t- 2 final diagnosis changed from NEC-II to NEC-I\n\t\t- 5 final diagnosis of Cow's Milk Protein Allergy\n\t\t- 1 intestinal anomaly with NEC-II\n* **Patient Chart Review**\n\t+ 167 patient charts reviewed for data extraction\n* **Patient Categorization**\n\t+ 33 NEC-III\n\t+ 33 NEC-II\n\t+ 101 Non-NEC controls matched with NEC-III in a 2-4:1 ratio for GA ± 1 week and date of birth ± 3 months\n\nThe flowchart provides a clear and concise overview of the patient selection process, highlighting the number of patients at each stage and the reasons for exclusion. The final categorization of patients into NEC-III, NEC-II, and Non-NEC controls is also presented.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nof UAC insertion after birth was signi /uniFB01 cantly higher in NEC -III (87.1%) than NEC -II (55.9%) and non -NEC (72.3%, p = 0.02). In addition, UAC remained in place longer than 5 days in NEC -III compared to NEC -II and non -NEC neonates (NEC -III 75%, NEC -II 36.4%, and non -NEC 47%, p = 0.007). X -ray con /uniFB01 rmed optimal UAC and UVC positions were not statistically different between the groups. We further examined the collinearity between UAC days and SNAPPE -II score in all the study neonates. SNAPPE -II scores were signi /uniFB01 -cantly higher at 48.1 (SD 18.6) in neonates who had UAC in place for &gt;5 days compared with infants with UAC in place for ≤ 5 days at 25.8 (SD 21.3), p ≤ 0.0001. Speci /uniFB01 cally, among neonates who developed NEC, SNAPPE -II scores were also signi /uniFB01 cantly higher in those who had UAC in place for &gt;5 days as opposed to those with UAC in place for ≤ 5 days (51 [SD 18.6] vs. 20.1 [SD 18.9]).\n\nMultivariate logistic regression analyses were performed to assess factors that were independently associated with NEC -III development. For this analysis, we created models that included all variables that were p &lt; 0.2 in bivariate analysis and performed logistic regression analysis. Most variables including SNAPPE -II scores did not remain in the /uniFB01 nal parsimonious regression model. After adjustment for known confounders, length of UAC greater than 5 days had 5.57 (1.65 -18.73) and 3.8 (1.05 -13.66) odds of developing NEC -III compared to NEC -II cases ( p = 0.006) and non -NEC controls (0.041), respectively (Table 3).\n\n## 4 | DISCUSSION\n\nIn this retrospective case -control study, we investigated the risk factors for NEC -III using Bell's criteria in preterm neonates born less than 33 weeks. We found duration of UAC over 5 days and rupture of membranes more than 7 days were signi /uniFB01 cantly associated with increased risk for NEC -III. NEC -III can have devastating outcomes in preterm neonates making it important for us to understand risk factors and possible preventive interventions. To the best of our knowledge, this is the /uniFB01 rst study to date that has looked at risk factors in particular the duration of umbilical lines in association with NEC -III development.\n\nThe incidence of NEC -III was higher in our study than what is reported in the literature. 8 This could be explained in part by the fact that our unit is one of the largest units in Canada that cares for a large proportion\n\n|\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nTABLE 1 Baseline characteristics comparing NEC -III, NEC -II, and non -NEC groups.\n\n|